WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.532
Gas balances are fragmented across networks.

2
00:00:02.532 --> 00:00:05.456
Funding wallets and swapping tokens add extra steps.

3
00:00:05.456 --> 00:00:09.770
For traders and AI agents, a missing gas balance can stall the
next transaction.

4
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.327
One gas token for every network.

5
00:00:12.327 --> 00:00:16.240
That is the goal behind MateX GAS and its smart-contract
architecture.

6
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.573
Token and vault contracts anchor the design.

7
00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:24.803
Funded sponsors would pay each network’s native gas, with
owner approvals and matched receipts.

8
00:00:24.803 --> 00:00:26.780
Broader dispatch remains in development.

9
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.168
Bring your own wallet and your own AI agent through the MCP
plugin.

10
00:00:31.168 --> 00:00:33.140
You review and approve each payment.

11
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:42.394
On Arc testnet, our internal conversion is now verified: one
test USDC paid for one hundred tMGAS.

12
00:00:42.394 --> 00:00:46.140
The buyer received the tokens, and both allowances finished at
zero.

13
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:53.156
Circle Facilitator Service supports our separate Arc USDC
checkout through x402.

14
00:00:53.156 --> 00:00:55.288
Buyer and seller approvals stay explicit.

15
00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:56.780
This flow is an illustration.

16
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:06.330
Our separate Base Sepolia record shows an agent-wallet
operation and a matched 0.004632 tMGAS charge, rechecked by
our team.

17
00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:09.321
Trading comes next.

18
00:01:09.321 --> 00:01:13.876
Inspect downloadable Bitcoin history, UTC coverage and exact
file checksums.

19
00:01:13.876 --> 00:01:16.780
These are historical inputs, not live trading results.

20
00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:23.770
Explore Ethereum and Solana perpetuals, mark prices and
funding history, with different-venue assumptions clearly
disclosed.

21
00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.815
Compare fixed strategies after fees and slippage, including
losing candidates.

22
00:01:28.815 --> 00:01:32.700
Read drawdown and tougher cost scenarios before drawing
conclusions.

23
00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:34.212
MateX GAS.

24
00:01:34.212 --> 00:01:36.962
One shared gas vision for users and agents.

25
00:01:36.962 --> 00:01:39.230
Explore the evidence, and build with us.
